WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.470
Film Courage: When you finish a screenplay
on spec, what’s protocol when you’re done?

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:08.791
Blayne Weaver: You finish it up and get all
of your buddies to read it and tell you what

00:00:08.791 --> 00:00:12.190
you think because this is like you only get
one first read on that.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:16.870
And this is hard for me because I like to
get it out there, see what the agent thinks

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:20.490
(as soon as possible) and…it’s a mistake!

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:24.460
You should hold it until it’s done.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:30.029
I have trouble taking that advice but…you
send it to your agent or producers or whoever

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:35.680
your contacts are in the world and you wait
to see what they say.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.559
It’s pretty simple.

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:41.540
You have very little power over it once they
take it.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:50.960
I have this one script that has had overwhelmingly
positive feedback and notes and has been at

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:57.750
one agency for about 8 months…you know…going
through actors, big movie stars reading it

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.280
or their “people” reading it.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:05.299
Anyways, we will wait for 9 weeks and then
we will hear “Well, Matthew McConaughey

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:07.189
just passed, but that’s okay.

00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:09.159
We’re going to Woody Harrelson.”

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:12.689
Okay…9 weeks later “Well, Woody Harrelson
passed, but that’s okay.

00:01:12.689 --> 00:01:14.319
We’re going to Josh Brolin.”

00:01:14.319 --> 00:01:18.299
And it just sits there and people read it
and you know, I do nothing.

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:25.090
I just sit and I’m happy that these agencies
and this talent has something of mine to read.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.219
You know, maybe they will see my name on it.

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:30.950
Film Courage: What if you don’t have the
agent or manager to give it to?

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:35.320
I mean, let’s say you are a new writer that
doesn’t have a foundation, hasn’t been

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.889
in Los Angeles that long?

00:01:36.889 --> 00:01:39.229
Blayne Weaver: There are online resources.

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:44.409
“The Blcklst” is one of them where you
can send in the script and have it covered,

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:45.409
have it read.

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:49.670
Coverage is this thing where it’s like a
book report on your script where they…all

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:53.090
the studios do that, they’ll give you a
little score, you know?

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:56.770
And you can pay people to do it professionally.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:02.420
But I would say that right now, these competitions,
like I know the Austin Film Festival has a

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:08.560
very prestigious one, but sending your script
in if you win a couple of awards, that’s

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:15.130
something that might get some leeway in sending
it to an agency.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.599
Normally I would say that almost never works.

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:21.960
I’ve never heard of anybody being read because
of a query letter.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.549
But if you have awards, maybe it would work?

00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:30.240
I mean look, there is no set way to do anything
in this business.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.560
Like everything is a twisty-turny, chutes
and ladders kind of thing.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.769
So you just try everything.

00:02:36.769 --> 00:02:41.330
You just throw everything at it and I mean
that’s how I finance movies.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:43.700
That’s how I got all of my movies done.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.630
You just try everything and see what works
out and be open to something working out in

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:49.139
a way that you didn’t expect.

